WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.830
Bat-fans are quickly forming a consensus opinion
about who they would like to see playing the

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.180
next Clown Prince of Crime.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.120
The roster of villains for the upcoming The
Batman may already be set, but it seems only

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.650
a matter of time before the next cinematic
incarnation of the Joker arrives to vex Robert

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.090
Pattinson's Dark Knight.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.160
The fan favorite choice for the role: Willem
Dafoe, who co-starred with Pattinson in last

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.560
year's Robert Eggers-directed psychological
horror flick The Lighthouse.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:31.600
"His coral-tined trident screeches banshee-like
in the tempest, and plunges right through

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.600
your gullet!"

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.860
Now, it's obvious that fans have no say in
casting Hollywood productions, and you never

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.480
know - The Batman just might flop spectacularly,
thereby failing to even earn a sequel.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.840
Having said that, we're going to go ahead
and file this fan-casting under "W" for "Why

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.040
the hell didn't we think of that?" - because
yeah, now that the notion of Dafoe as the

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.680
Joker is out there, it certainly seems like
the definition of a no-brainer.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.540
The veteran actor is possessed of a wiry physicality
which is perfect for the role; his facial

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:02.260
structure and toothy grin are also custom-made
for Jokerdom, as evidenced by some twisted

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.710
fan art courtesy of Instagram user thesithlordjoker.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:09.680
His role in The Lighthouse demonstrated in
one fell swoop that he has incredible chemistry

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.940
with Pattinson, and also that his skill at
portraying wild-eyed craziness as an actor

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.780
has in no way diminished over the years.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.820
To top it all off, he already has experience
filling the shoes of an iconic supervillain.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.300
But we'll get to that in a bit.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.420
Fans on Twitter have been psyching themselves
up to see Willem play "da foe" of Pattinson's

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.900
Batman - see what we did there? - ever since
The Lighthouse dropped last October.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.460
The flick is essentially a two-man stage production
with Dafoe and Pattinson continually one-upping

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.350
each other in the crazy department, and the
two actors' intense chemistry had light bulbs

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.020
popping up over the heads of Bat-fans far
and wide.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:43.360
Twitter user @SEALkraven set the scene for
the idea, writing:

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.820
"Willem Dafoe as Joker, in a post-credit scene
of The Batman.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.360
Just imagine."

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.320
Other users asserted that the Joker was nothing
less than the role that Dafoe was born to

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.100
play, and some expressed their shock that
such a spot-on casting choice had never come

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.120
to fruition before.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:00.329
Oddly enough, it turns out that the idea of
casting Dafoe as the Clown Prince of Crime

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:03.820
has been had before - by the guy who wrote
the screenplay that would evolve into Tim

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.070
Burton's Batman.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.039
In an interview with The Hollywood Reporter
in 2018, Dafoe recalled that Sam Hamm - who

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:12.820
wrote the initial drafts of Batman - had talked
him up for the role of the Joker, which of

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.640
course eventually went to Jack Nicholson.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.640
Dafoe recalled:

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.319
"Hamm said something about how physi­cally
I would be perfect for the part, but they

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:20.349
never offered it to me."

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.690
Perhaps inevitably, though, Dafoe got his
chance to portray a cackling, unhinged supervillain

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.610
when he was cast as Norman Osborn's Green
Goblin in Sam Raimi's Spider-Man in 2002,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.390
making his mark on one of the films that gave
birth to the modern superhero genre.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.620
“We are who we choose to be… now, choose!"

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.120
“NO!"

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.460
Of course, he absolutely crushed the role
- although Raimi and screenwriter David Koepp

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.290
made the same mistake Hamm and script doctor
Warren Skaaren had made with Batman, killing

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.620
off the iconic villain in his very first appearance.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.819
Since then, Dafoe has dipped his toes in the
superhero pool - so to speak - in 2018's Aquaman,

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.360
in which he popped up as the titular character's
friend and adviser Vulko.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.700
This would probably not preclude him from
taking on the role of the Joker, though, as

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.780
many fans have theorized that The Batman and
its potential sequels will be set in a different

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.269
universe than the main DCEU timeline.

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:13.080
We would sure love for this bit of fan casting
to come to fruition, and we hope the people

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.540
in charge consider actually making the man
an offer.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.760
Hey, Warner Bros.: You have the power to make
Bat-fans the world over freak out just by

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.970
giving the guy a call.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:21.680
How about it?

